+# Agent Note: Reuse the Client object graph in non-browser product shells
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-14-non-browser-client-object-runtime.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The Web Client's Connection, generated Remote assembly, and Session objects own physical recovery, logical stream reopening, gap repair, paging, queue reconciliation, projection ordering, and submission retirement. A non-browser editor integration that handwrites the Web wire and reimplements those state machines creates a second Client Harness whose behavior can diverge from DSH.
+
+The published `/client` artifacts are dynamic browser module-table rows rather than Node.js ESM modules. Importing them directly from a Node product shell executes a `window.__ModuleLoader__` registration, while mounting source entries independently makes the shell reconstruct DSH's private plugin graph and transport ordering.
+
+## Decision
+
+`@deepseek-ai/dsh-api-client-runtime` is the Node.js ESM entry for non-browser product shells connecting to an authenticated loopback DSH Web Host. It bundles the existing Client implementations and generated Remote contributions into one library artifact, mounts the Typert registry, Connection, Gateway, API Remotes, and Session Controller in their Cordis graph, and returns the canonical `connection`, `remote`, and `sessions` faces. It loads no React or presentation plugins.
+
+The caller supplies the exact loopback origin and the authority-bound browser cookie. The runtime maps Connection's internal HTTP targets to that origin, adds the cookie and matching Origin header, and gives `RemoteStreamMuxClient` an authenticated `ws` socket factory for `/api/remote.mux`. Connection still mints every RPC identity; Gateway still owns multiplexing and logical streams; Session Controller still owns Session list, queue, projection, follow, paging, and reconciliation behavior.
+
+`RemoteStreamMuxClient` therefore accepts an optional exact URL and socket factory. Browser construction remains the default and uses the page authority plus global WebSocket. The configurable physical constructor does not expose Remote stream frames or move logical-stream ownership into the shell.
+
+The runtime temporarily installs the existing shell transport hook while the Connection plugin mounts. It rejects overlapping graph assembly during that interval and removes the hook before other plugins mount. Connected runtimes are independent after construction. Disposal tears down the complete Cordis root and then closes the shell-owned stream mux.
+
+File upload remains unavailable in this Node runtime because the existing implementation owns browser Blob Worker and XMLHttpRequest behavior. The runtime provides an explicit unavailable service so Session object composition remains complete without pretending to upload.
+
+## Ownership
+
+| Owner | Responsibility |
+|---|---|
+| Product shell | DSH process lifecycle, launch-token exchange, cookie secret storage, and UI projection |
+| API Client Runtime | Node carrier construction and complete React-free Client graph lifecycle |
+| Connection | RPC identity, unary envelopes, generation state, and retry policy |
+| API Gateway | physical mux behavior, logical streams, cancellation, and generated Remote dispatch |
+| Session Controller Client | list races, Session identities, queue mirrors, projections, pending submissions, history windows, paging, and gap repair |
+| DSH Host | authoritative Agent, Session, queue, approval, command, and persistence behavior |
+
+## Verification
+
+A Client-package composition spec starts real HTTP and WebSocket fixture carriers, requires cookie and Origin headers on both channels, opens the forwarded-event generation and Session control baseline, obtains a ready empty Session list, exercises the unavailable upload face, and disposes twice. The same spec covers authority, cookie, cancellation, partial-assembly cleanup, and overlapping assembly refusal. Gateway coverage constructs the mux through an explicit shell URL and socket factory. A built-library smoke connects the packaged artifact to `dsh web` before a product consumes the npm path.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Let each product shell implement the Web wire.** This duplicates rpcId correlation, Remote stream multiplexing, recovery, Session paging, queue ordering, and projection races. DSH would remain the Host authority while no longer owning the behavior that makes its Client projection coherent.
+
+**Publish every browser `/client` entry as a second Node artifact.** A shell would still need to know the complete plugin roster, activation order, transport hook lifetime, and required placeholder services. One assembly package keeps that composition inside DSH and permits internal package rearrangement without changing every shell.
+
+**Use the automation SDK profile instead of the Web Client objects.** The SDK owns request-scoped automation turns over stdio and does not project the Web Session list, live control baselines, paged event windows, pending submissions, or approval interactions required by an interactive editor.
+
+**Import DSH Web presentation packages.** This would couple an editor to React, Slots, browser navigation, and DSH's visual composition. The shell needs canonical data objects and operations, not the Web UI.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Non-browser product shells can replace handwritten Session transport and synchronization incrementally while retaining their own presentation. The npm artifact is larger because it closes over generated codecs and the Client object implementations, but the shell no longer selects or versions those internals independently. Cookie authentication and loopback Web deployment remain required. Node file upload needs a separate carrier implementation before shells can expose attachments. The runtime's Session service retains Web navigation concepts such as `current`, while shells may open and retain several Session bindings and keep tab selection in their own presentation state.
